      "math_academy_id": "e-144",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = 2x^3 - 2x$ at the point $(1,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x) = 2x^3 - 2x.$ Computing the derivative $f'(x)$ gives\n\n$$\nf'(x)  = 6x^2-2.\n$$\n\nWe then compute $f'(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\nf'(1)  = 6(1)^2-2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=4$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,0),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =4(x-1) \\\\ y & =4x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
